I was the original poster, although I stil maintain that the technique
is "abuse" - but this is an arguable point

>The normal limit that a nethack weapon can be enchanted to _with
>guaranteed success_ is +8, because any enchantment of a weapon that is
>+6 or more has a 33% chance of overevaporating the weapon. 

No, it has a 66.6% chance of evaporating.  That's why it's important
to separate the swords in 4 piles, since you'll tend to lose the piles.

>
>Normally, nethack only allows you to have one artifact in a given
>game, so if you overenchant one, it's gone for the rest of that game.
> [ much stuff deleted ]
>Once you have the zillion copies of one of these three artifacts (I
>recommand Excalibur since it's by far the best weapon) we can then go
>about enchanting it to +20. Let us assume that we #dipped 64 +8
>longswords into a fountain and now have 64 blessed +8 rustproof
>longswords named Excalibur (in one inventory slot, this is important).

The other way to do all this is to get the +20 long sword first - and
then turn it into Excalibur.  Among other things, this will allow you
to start with more long swords, because you aren't limited to what you
can carry, but to 1/4th of what you can carry.  In other words, if the
max you can carry is 50 longswords, you can make 4 groups of 50 to
start off - the carry limit is important for inventory grouping.

On other point: this way doesn't leave lots of +15
Excaliburs lying around for some ambitious gnome lord to pick up....

=>Let's also assume that our blessed enchant weapon scrolls will fail
=>exactly 1/3 of the time. Now, basically what one does is drop half of
=>the copies they have in case the ones they are enchanting evaporate.
=>
=>Drop 32 of the 64 longswords.
=>Enchant the 32 we are still wielding -> they evaporate.
=>Pick up the 32 longswords on the ground, and drop 16 of them again.
=>Enchant the 16 we still have         -> they are now all +11.
=>Drop 8 of the ones we have on the ground.
=>Enchant the 8 we still have          -> they are now all +14
=>Drop 4 of the ones we have on the ground.
=>Enchant the 4 we still have          -> they evaporate.
=>Pick of the 4 we have left on the ground, and drop 2 of them again.
=>Enchant the 2 we still have          -> they are now both +17
=>Drop one of the ones we have on the ground.
=>Enchant the one we still have        -> It is now a +20 Excalibur!!!

The technique is basically correct, but you must use piles of four
because the enchantments will fail 2/3 of the time.  E.g. start with
4 piles of 50.  Let's say that sfter two piles evaporate, the third
makes it to +11 - now divide into 4 piles of 12 or 13.  Let's say that
luck is not kind and the first three piles evaporate - OK split the last
pile into 4 piles of 3 - hah! now perhaps we get lucky and get two
straight enchantments to succeed, we have 3 longswords at +17 - hopefully
one can make it to +20.  If not, +17 Excalibur is a nice consolation prize.

Actually, the game is boring with a +20 Excalibur - way too easy, but then
it's way too easy if you polypile anyway.


